Fixed and verified. Here's what was actually wrong.

## The cause

Not a permissions problem, not a `PATH` problem. Your system Python 3.13 at `/usr/local` had its **`pip` package directory deleted while its metadata was left behind**:

```
/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/
├── README.txt
└── pip-24.3.1.dist-info/    ← metadata present, RECORD lists 852 files
                               pip/ (the actual code) — MISSING
```

The `/usr/local/bin/pip3` script was intact and just did `from pip._internal.cli.main import main`, which is why every invocation died with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`. This is the classic signature of a half-finished uninstall, a `rm -rf` of the package dir, or a container/image build step that stripped it.

## The trap that made the obvious fix fail

`python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` **silently did nothing**:

```
Requirement already satisfied: pip in .../site-packages (24.3.1)
```

`ensurepip` consults installed metadata to decide whether to act. The orphaned `dist-info` asserted pip 24.3.1 was installed, so the installer skipped the work — leaving you exactly as broken as before, with a success exit code. Worth remembering: `ensurepip` reporting success is not evidence that pip exists.

## The fix

1. Moved the lying metadata aside: `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` → `/tmp/pip-orphan-backup/`
2. `/usr/local/bin/python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip` — this time it installed from the wheel bundled inside CPython itself, so **no network access was needed** to repair pip.

## Verification

I wrote the checks as a script that fails non-zero, at `.gnomon-out/verify_pip.sh` (that path is gitignored, so it won't be committed):

```
PASS  python3 imports pip
PASS  pip3 reports a version
PASS  pip (bare name) works
PASS  python -m pip works
PASS  dependency graph intact
PASS  pip package dir is real
PASS  exactly one pip dist-info
PASS  can resolve deps from PyPI

ALL CHECKS PASSED   (exit 0)
```

Beyond the script, a real end-to-end install into a throwaway venv: `pip install packaging` → `import packaging` → `26.3`. `pip check` reports `No broken requirements found.` I used venvs for these tests deliberately — I did not install third-party packages into the system site-packages just to prove it works. `--dry-run` confirmed the system pip can also resolve a full dependency tree (`requests` → certifi, charset-normalizer, idna, urllib3).

## Two things to know

**A second, separate Python exists and still has no pip.** `/usr/bin/python3` is Python **3.11.2**, distinct from the 3.13.1 you were hitting. It has no pip either, but I left it alone on purpose: it carries `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED`, so it's a distro-managed interpreter where system-wide `pip install` is blocked by design. Using `pip` against it is the wrong move — use `python3 -m venv` there. Your 3.13 has no such marker, so it accepts installs.

**Your pip is 24.3.1 and 26.2.2 is available.** I did not upgrade it, since that wasn't the problem and upgrading a system pip is a change you may want to make deliberately. When you do, prefer `python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip` in a user or venv context.

Root cause is worth a second look on your side: pip's code was removed but its metadata wasn't, so whatever deleted it ran non-atomically. If that was an image build or a provisioning script, it will likely do the same thing again on the next run.

